# Groop Groop Roster Generator (offline Excel version)

Generates the weekly cell serving roster (Prayer, Word, Worship Vocals,
Worship Instru) from an Excel copy of the Google Form responses plus the
committee's session schedule. No Google account or internet needed to run it.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 1. What's in this folder

    groop-groop-roster/
    ├── README               <- this file
    ├── roster_local.py      <- the script you run (offline Excel version)
    ├── roster.py            <- rostering rules + settings (REQUIRED - roster_local.py uses it)
    ├── requirements.txt     <- Python libraries to install
    ├── input/
    │   └── responses.xlsx   <- YOU put the downloaded Excel file here
    └── output/
        └── roster_output.xlsx  <- the script creates this

Always copy the WHOLE folder. roster_local.py will not run without roster.py
next to it.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 2. One-time setup on a new computer

### a) Install Python (version 3.10 or newer)

Windows:
  1. Download from https://www.python.org/downloads/
  2. Run the installer and TICK "Add python.exe to PATH" on the first screen.
  3. Open a new Command Prompt / PowerShell and check it works:

        python --version

Mac:
  1. Download from https://www.python.org/downloads/ and install.
  2. Use `python3` and `pip3` wherever this README says `python` and `pip`.

### b) Install the required library

Open a terminal IN THIS FOLDER (Windows: open the folder in File Explorer,
click the address bar, type `cmd`, press Enter), then run:

    pip install -r requirements.txt

This installs:
  - openpyxl  - reads/writes Excel files (needed for roster_local.py)
  - gspread   - only needed for the Google Sheets version (roster.py);
                harmless to install. To skip it: `pip install openpyxl`

You only need to do this once per computer.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 3. Preparing the input file

### a) Download the Google Sheet as Excel

Open the Google Sheet linked to the form, then:
File -> Download -> Microsoft Excel (.xlsx)

Rename the downloaded file to `responses.xlsx` and save it in the `input/`
folder (replace the old one if it exists).

### b) The file must contain two tabs

1. `Form Responses 1` - created automatically by Google Forms. Don't edit
   the header row. The script finds columns by these words in the question
   text, so keep them in the questions:

       Question                                   Must contain
       ----------------------------------------   ---------------
       Name                                       "name"
       Birthday                                   "birthday"
       Able to serve for cell sessions weekly in  "weekly"
       Other serving opportunities in cell        "other serving"
       Preferred serving frequency                "frequency"
       Misc comments                              "comment"
       Block out dates (x10)                      "block"

   Serving area options must be spelled exactly:
   Prayer, Word, Worship (Vocals), Worship (Instru)

   If someone submits the form more than once, their LATEST response is used.

2. `Schedule` - filled in by the committee, one row per week:

       Date        | Prayer | Word | Worship (Vocals) | Worship (Instru) | Notes
       ------------|--------|------|------------------|------------------|-------------
       07/01/2027  | Y      | Y    | Y                | Y                |
       14/01/2027  | Y      | Y    |                  |                  | Prayer night
       21/01/2027  |        |      |                  |                  | CNY - no cell

   - Date as dd/mm/yyyy (or a real Excel date).
   - Mark a role as needed with Y / Yes / X / TRUE / 1 (ticked checkboxes work).
   - A row with nothing marked = no session that week (Notes is shown on the roster).
   - Column headers must match the role names exactly.

If the schedule is kept in a SEPARATE Excel file, see "Options" below.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 4. Running it

In a terminal in this folder:

    python roster_local.py

It reads   input/responses.xlsx
and writes output/roster_output.xlsx

You'll see something like:

    18 members, 26 weeks
    Wrote ...\output\roster_output.xlsx

If any slot couldn't be filled it also says how many - check the Notes column.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 5. The output

`output/roster_output.xlsx` has two tabs:

- **Roster** - one row per week: Date, who's on each role, Notes.
    - `⚠ UNFILLED` = nobody available (everyone blocked out or already serving
      that week). The committee needs to fill this manually or drop the role.
    - Notes also flag anyone rostered near their birthday because there was no
      one else.
- **Load Summary** - per person: times served in each role, total effort
  points, and an approximate fair-share target based on their preferred
  frequency. Use this to sanity-check fairness.

Each run OVERWRITES output/roster_output.xlsx. Rename or move the old file
first if you want to keep it. Your input file is never modified.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 6. Rostering rules

Hard rules (never broken):
  - Never rostered on any of their block-out dates.
  - Only rostered for areas they ticked on the form.
  - Never rostered for two areas in the same week.

Soft rules (followed unless impossible; flagged in Notes when broken):
  - Not rostered within 3 days either side of their birthday.

Balancing:
  - Word counts as 2 effort points; all other areas count as 1.
  - Load is shared in proportion to preferred frequency (someone who chose
    "1 month" serves about twice as often as someone who chose "2 months").
  - Tries not to roster someone again before their preferred gap has passed.
  - Same input always gives the same roster.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 7. Options

    python roster_local.py "C:\path\to\other file.xlsx"     use a different input file
    python roster_local.py --schedule-file input\schedule.xlsx   schedule in its own file
    python roster_local.py --responses-tab "Sheet1"          different responses tab name
    python roster_local.py --schedule-tab "Dates"            different schedule tab name
    python roster_local.py --output output\roster_2027.xlsx  save under another name

Options can be combined.

Settings you can change at the top of roster.py (open in any text editor):
  - EFFORT                 effort points per area (Word = 2)
  - BIRTHDAY_BUFFER_DAYS   days either side of birthday to avoid (default 3)
  - RANDOM_SEED            change the number to get a different (still fair) roster
  - RESPONSES_TAB / SCHEDULE_TAB   default tab names

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 8. Troubleshooting

| Message / problem                                   | Fix |
|-----------------------------------------------------|-----|
| `'python' is not recognized` / `Python was not found` | Python not installed or not on PATH - reinstall and tick "Add to PATH". On Mac use `python3`. |
| `No module named 'openpyxl'`                        | Run `pip install -r requirements.txt` again. |
| `No module named 'roster'`                          | roster.py is missing - copy it into this folder. |
| `Input file not found`                              | Save the Excel file as `input/responses.xlsx`, or pass its path. |
| `has no tab named ...`                              | Rename the tab in Excel, or use `--responses-tab` / `--schedule-tab`. |
| `Could not save ... close it in Excel`              | Close roster_output.xlsx in Excel and run again. |
| `Schedule tab needs a Date column ...`              | Check the Schedule headers match section 3b exactly. |
| `could not parse date '...' - ignored`              | A date isn't dd/mm/yyyy - fix that cell. |
| Someone never gets rostered                         | Check they ticked serving areas and their name isn't blank. |
| `TypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for |`      | Python is older than 3.10 - install a newer version. |
